[Jay Haynes: ‘Apple’s $3 Trillion Valuation’](https://web.archive.org/web/20140819113945/http://www.jayhaynes.net/2014/01/apples-3-trillion-valuation.html)

Jay Haynes:

> If we assume that Apple will grow its owner earnings at 5% for the
> next 10 years, and then 2% for all years after that (with
> adjustments for cash and debt), Apple’s market cap wouldn’t be
> $453 billion. It wouldn’t even be $1.2 trillion. It would be $3
> trillion. This is a share price of $3,275 in contrast to today’s
> share price of $506. At just 5% annual growth for Apple.

That doesn’t mean the market is wrong. It just means the market thinks Apple’s growth is over, that it won’t even manage single-digit growth for the next decade.

**Update, May 2026:** Haynes’s original blog went belly-up (perhaps when Typepad did?), alas, but [he republished the piece on Medium](https://medium.com/thrv/the-math-behind-warren-buffets-1-billion-stake-in-apple-5cbcc228dada), with a bit of additional commentary up front, in 2016. This came up in the context of [my revisiting Haynes’s prediction that Apple would reach a $3 trillion valuation](https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/05/25/awarding-jay-haynes-his-being-right-points).

